WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.453 --> 00:00:16.239
The next day Charley and the Dodger went out to work as usual.

2
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.762
Fagin took the opportunity to lecture Oliver on the sin of ingratitude.

3
00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:24.744
The old man clearly thought Oliver was guilty of that sin.

4
00:00:24.764 --> 00:00:28.647
"Remember who took you in, my dear," said Fagin.

5
00:00:28.667 --> 00:00:30.568
"I cherished you.

6
00:00:31.584 --> 00:00:34.585
Without my timely aid, you might have perished from hunger."

7
00:00:35.705 --> 00:00:38.186
Fagin smiled hideously and patted Oliver's head.

8
00:00:38.206 --> 00:00:44.388
"But if you behave and apply yourself to business, there's a chance we'll still be very good friends."

9
00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:47.388
Oliver did not want to be friends with Fagin.

10
00:00:48.489 --> 00:00:52.750
Taking his hat and wrapping himself in an old, patched coat, Fagin left the room.

11
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.251
Oliver heard him lock the door.

12
00:00:56.371 --> 00:00:59.652
And so Oliver remained all that day and for many more days.

13
00:01:01.492 --> 00:01:04.314
He saw no one between the early morning hours and midnight.

14
00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:10.058
He was alone with his thoughts, and those always turned toward his kind friends—Mr.

15
00:01:10.098 --> 00:01:11.099
Brownlow and Mrs.

16
00:01:11.139 --> 00:01:14.181
Bedwin. Oliver was miserable indeed.

17
00:01:15.282 --> 00:01:19.865
After about a week, Fagin left the door to the room unlocked, so Oliver was free to wander

18
00:01:19.926 --> 00:01:20.626
about the house.

19
00:01:21.827 --> 00:01:27.951
Looking at the ornate fireplaces and intricately carved woodwork, Oliver concluded that the house had once belonged to

20
00:01:27.991 --> 00:01:28.572
better people.

21
00:01:29.772 --> 00:01:31.973
But now it was a dismal and dreary place.

22
00:01:33.273 --> 00:01:37.614
Spiders had built their webs in the corners, and mice sometimes scampered across the floor.

23
00:01:38.694 --> 00:01:43.856
The shutters were closed tight over all the windows, so only tiny cracks of light entered the rooms.

24
00:01:45.056 --> 00:01:48.877
On the top floor, there was one room with bars over the window instead of shutters.

25
00:01:50.078 --> 00:01:52.978
Oliver often gazed out this window for hours at a time.

26
00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:58.680
But all he could see were the roofs of other houses and their blackened chimney tops.

27
00:01:59.884 --> 00:02:02.486
One afternoon Charley and the Dodger were making plans.

28
00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:05.347
"Oh, Oliver," called the Dodger.

29
00:02:05.367 --> 00:02:09.810
"My boots need cleaning before I can go out this evening like a proper gent.

30
00:02:10.851 --> 00:02:11.831
Come here and clean them."

31
00:02:12.892 --> 00:02:15.694
Oliver was so lonely that he was glad to make himself useful.

32
00:02:16.754 --> 00:02:21.097
The Dodger sat on the kitchen table while Oliver knelt on the floor, holding the Dodger's foot in

33
00:02:21.137 --> 00:02:21.557
his lap.

34
00:02:22.598 --> 00:02:23.938
Charley stood around and watched.

35
00:02:23.958 --> 00:02:26.320
"Why won't you work for Fagin?"

36
00:02:26.624 --> 00:02:29.646
Charley asked as Oliver scraped the mud from the Dodger's left boot.

37
00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:33.808
"You'd make a fortune," said the Dodger, swinging his right leg.

38
00:02:33.828 --> 00:02:37.030
"I don't like it," Oliver said timidly.

39
00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.051
"I wish he'd let me go.

40
00:02:39.071 --> 00:02:41.552
I I'd rather go."

41
00:02:41.572 --> 00:02:46.395
"And Fagin would rather not let you go," said Charley with a laugh.

42
00:02:47.555 --> 00:02:52.258
Oliver knew this was true, but he thought it might be dangerous to express his feelings more openly.

43
00:02:53.279 --> 00:02:55.760
So he only sighed and went on with his boot cleaning.

44
00:02:56.772 --> 00:02:58.233
"Come on," said Charley.

45
00:02:58.253 --> 00:03:01.155
"You know we're the only friends you've got now."

46
00:03:01.175 --> 00:03:03.156
"Some friends."

47
00:03:04.276 --> 00:03:05.297
Oliver stopped working.

48
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:09.019
"You ran off and let me get punished for something you did."

49
00:03:09.039 --> 00:03:11.541
"Had to," said the Dodger.

50
00:03:11.561 --> 00:03:14.202
"The police know we work for Fagin.

51
00:03:15.303 --> 00:03:17.224
He might have gotten into trouble if we'd been caught."

52
00:03:18.265 --> 00:03:21.526
Oliver didn't say anything as he finished polishing the Dodger's boots.

53
00:03:21.546 --> 00:03:23.788
"You know what your problem is?"

54
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.642
said the Dodger as he admired his shiny black boots.

55
00:03:26.662 --> 00:03:29.804
"You've not been brought up properly.

56
00:03:30.825 --> 00:03:32.346
But Fagin will make something of you.

57
00:03:33.366 --> 00:03:36.148
And if you don't steal watches and handkerchiefs, someone else will.

58
00:03:37.329 --> 00:03:39.270
So you might as well do it and get the money."

59
00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:44.354
"That's all true," said Fagin, who had entered the room unseen by Oliver.

60
00:03:44.374 --> 00:03:47.556
"Now, everyone, come sit by the fire."

61
00:03:48.697 --> 00:03:53.420
The boys pulled some chairs close to the fire, and Fagin insisted that Oliver sit next to him.

62
00:03:54.572 --> 00:03:59.815
Fagin was so charming that night and his stories were so amusing that Oliver found himself laughing heartily

63
00:03:59.935 --> 00:04:01.256
despite his better judgment.

64
00:04:02.316 --> 00:04:06.599
From that day Oliver was seldom left alone but spent many hours with the two boys.

65
00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:12.342
They all took turns playing their old game with Fagin, who also entertained them with stories of robberies

66
00:04:12.382 --> 00:04:13.563
he'd committed years ago.

67
00:04:15.344 --> 00:04:20.146
For Oliver, anything was better than being left alone with only his own sad thoughts for company.

68
00:04:21.287 --> 00:04:22.868
Fagin was slowly winning him over.

69
00:04:24.068 --> 00:04:27.611
One cold, damp night, Fagin left the boys at home while he went out.

70
00:04:28.651 --> 00:04:31.113
He had business to discuss with Bill Sikes at his house.

71
00:04:32.334 --> 00:04:33.334
Nancy opened the door.

72
00:04:34.475 --> 00:04:36.417
Sikes and his dog were sitting by the fire.

73
00:04:36.437 --> 00:04:39.258
"What's happening with the house in Chertsey?

74
00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:42.241
When are you and Toby Crackit going to rob it?"

75
00:04:42.641 --> 00:04:44.702
asked Fagin once Nancy had brought him a drink.

76
00:04:45.843 --> 00:04:46.704
Sikes shook his head.

77
00:04:46.724 --> 00:04:49.806
"Toby's been hanging about the place for two weeks.

78
00:04:50.966 --> 00:04:52.868
He can't bribe any of the servants to help us.

79
00:04:54.036 --> 00:04:56.697
We might still be able to do it, but we'd need a small boy."

80
00:04:56.717 --> 00:04:59.998
"Oh, so you're going to slip him through a window?"

81
00:05:00.398 --> 00:05:01.058
asked Fagin.

82
00:05:01.078 --> 00:05:04.279
"Never you mind what we're going to do.

83
00:05:05.439 --> 00:05:06.500
Just get me a boy."

84
00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.680
"Oliver is a small boy," said Nancy.

85
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:15.342
"That he is," said Fagin, "and it's time he started working for his bread.

86
00:05:16.442 --> 00:05:18.903
He'll do anything you want as long as you frighten him enough."

87
00:05:18.923 --> 00:05:23.224
"Oh, I'll frighten him," said Sikes.

88
00:05:23.244 --> 00:05:24.685
"You don't need to worry about that."

89
00:05:24.705 --> 00:05:28.028
"Good!" said Fagin enthusiastically.

90
00:05:28.048 --> 00:05:32.952
"Once we fill his mind with the idea that he has been a thief, he's ours.

91
00:05:34.093 --> 00:05:35.174
He'll be ours for life!"

92
00:05:36.355 --> 00:05:38.737
Fagin crossed his arms and hugged himself with joy.

93
00:05:38.757 --> 00:05:41.399
"Ours!" said Sikes.

94
00:05:41.419 --> 00:05:43.381
"Yours, you mean."

95
00:05:43.401 --> 00:05:45.462
"Perhaps I do."

96
00:05:45.482 --> 00:05:50.446
"And what makes you take such pains with one pale kid when there are fifty boys on the

97
00:05:50.486 --> 00:05:52.128
streets of London that you might choose from?"

98
00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.361
"Because they're of no use to me.

99
00:05:56.461 --> 00:05:58.682
Their looks convict them when they get into trouble.

100
00:05:59.622 --> 00:06:01.603
But this boy appears so innocent.

101
00:06:02.784 --> 00:06:06.165
If I properly manage him Fagin gazed into the fire.

102
00:06:06.185 --> 00:06:08.486
"Never mind why I want him.

103
00:06:09.587 --> 00:06:11.487
Now when are you going to rob this house in Chertsey?"

104
00:06:12.608 --> 00:06:16.870
After some discussion it was agreed that Nancy would go to Fagin's the next night and bring Oliver

105
00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:17.690
back to Sikes.

106
00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.292
Oliver would trust her because she had protected him when Fagin wanted to beat him.

107
00:06:23.476 --> 00:06:25.617
The following day they would set out for Chertsey.

108
00:06:26.617 --> 00:06:29.598
With their arrangements settled, Fagin went out into the night.

109
00:06:30.678 --> 00:06:33.539
When he reached his gloomy home, the Dodger answered the door.

110
00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:35.559
"Is Oliver in bed?

111
00:06:35.579 --> 00:06:39.841
I want to speak to him," said Fagin as they went into the kitchen.

112
00:06:40.881 --> 00:06:43.041
"Here he is," said the Dodger softly.

113
00:06:44.182 --> 00:06:46.262
Oliver was lying fast asleep on the floor.

114
00:06:47.383 --> 00:06:49.643
His face was pale with anxiety and sadness.

115
00:06:49.663 --> 00:06:53.449
"Not now, not yet," murmured Fagin.

116
00:06:53.469 --> 00:06:55.953
"I'll wait until tomorrow."
